// ============================================================================
// Research ethics register (single list) — every project this service refers
// to a partner HREC, on one row.
// Render the ERD by pasting this file into https://dbdiagram.io
//
// WHY ONE LIST. The obvious shape for this domain is three: a project, its
// amendments, its progress reports, joined by Lookups. That shape is refused
// here for a measured reason. A SharePoint Lookup picker enumerates its whole
// target list and there is NO way to restrict what it offers — the field has
// no filtering attribute, the calculated-column workaround is refused past
// 5,000 items, and the target list's own default view filter is ignored by
// the picker. All three were measured on a live tenant; see the "SharePoint
// cannot filter a lookup" block in website/docs/reference/mapping.md.
//
// A project register is an ACCUMULATOR: it only grows. By year three the
// picker on an amendment form offers every long-closed project alongside the
// live one, and staff cannot find theirs. The list never approaches 5,000
// items — 5 to 30 new projects a year — so this is not the item threshold.
// It is picker usability, and it has no fix inside a list schema.
//
// The insight the whole design turns on: FILTERING WORKS EVERYWHERE IN
// SHAREPOINT EXCEPT A LOOKUP PICKER. Remove the child lists and the
// accumulation problem becomes one SharePoint solves well — a default view
// filtered to live projects. That is what ProjectStage and the "Live
// projects" view are for, and they are load-bearing rather than housekeeping.
//
// WHAT IT COSTS is stated rather than hidden, here and in README.md and
// 50-govern/governance.md: there are no per-amendment and no per-report rows,
// so the register cannot answer "which projects filed late in 2025", and two
// simultaneous in-flight amendments are not representable. The partner HREC
// holds the authoritative file either way.
//
// TWO GATES, NEVER MERGED. Ethics approval (the partner committee's decision)
// and site authorisation (this service's own) are separate states with
// separate authorities, references and dates. A project can be
// ethics-approved and not authorised here, and that is the operationally
// dangerous state this register exists to make visible. SiteReadiness
// collapses the pair into one answer and costs NO join: both gates are Choice
// columns on this same table, which a SharePoint calculated column may read.
//
// NO PARTICIPANT DATA, at any grain. Not names, not identifiers, not
// aggregate recruitment counts. See 50-govern/governance.md.
// ============================================================================

Project research_ethics_register_simple { database_type: 'SharePoint Online Lists' }

// What kind of activity this is. The register holds research AND the quality
// activities beside it, because the question "did this need ethics review,
// and who decided?" is asked of both.
Enum project_type {
  "Research"
  "Quality improvement"
  "Clinical audit"
  "Evaluation"
  "Student project"
}

// The review pathway. The National Statement (2025) sorts risk into four
// profiles: minimal (no risk of harm or discomfort, at worst minor burden or
// inconvenience) and low (no risk of harm, risk of discomfort) are the lower
// pair; greater than low (risk of harm) and high (risk of significant harm)
// are the higher pair. Greater than low risk must go to an HREC; no more than
// low risk may be reviewed under other processes.
//
// The LNR member keeps its name on purpose. "Negligible risk" is the wording
// of editions before 2025 -- the current one says "minimal" -- but the state
// review PATHWAYS, their application forms and their committees are still
// named LNR, and this column records which pathway a project took, not which
// national risk category it sits in. An operator picks what their committee's
// form says. If your partner committee has renamed its pathway, rename this
// member to match; nothing in the build depends on the wording.
//
// Sources are cited in 50-govern/governance.md, by what they say rather than
// by paragraph number.
//
// The last member is not a loophole. An organisation is expected to have a
// process for deciding when a quality assurance or evaluation activity needs
// ethics review, and recording that the decision was made, by whom and when,
// is the audit answer.
Enum ethics_pathway {
  "Not yet determined"
  "Greater than low risk (full HREC review)"
  "Low or negligible risk (LNR review)"
  "Quality assurance or evaluation - no ethics review"
}

// An attribute of the STUDY DESIGN, not of any participant. It is one of the
// triggers for considering ethics review, and it is what makes the pathway
// choice above defensible on the row.
Enum participant_involvement {
  "No contact with patients or staff"
  "Existing data or records only"
  "Staff participants only"
  "Patient or client participants"
}

// GATE 1 — the partner committee's decision.
//
// "Response required" is the only member that says the ball is with US rather
// than with the committee, which is why it exists rather than being folded
// into "Under review". The "Response required" view is built on it.
//
// "Ethics review not required" is the recorded outcome of the quality or
// evaluation determination above, and the save rule on site authorisation
// treats it as an ethics clearance, because for such an activity it is one.
//
// "Expired" is maintained by the expiry sweep in 50-govern/governance.md.
// Nothing computes it: a SharePoint calculated column cannot read [Today].
Enum ethics_status {
  "Not yet submitted"
  "Submitted"
  "Under review"
  "Response required"
  "Approved"
  "Not approved"
  "Ethics review not required"
  "Withdrawn"
  "Expired"
}

// Conditions are orthogonal to the decision: a project is approved AND its
// conditions are outstanding, or approved and there were none. Folding them
// into ethics_status would make "Approved" mean two different things.
Enum conditions_status {
  "None"
  "Outstanding"
  "Met"
}

// GATE 2 — this service's own authorisation, on the research governance
// officer's site-specific assessment. Evidence of ethics approval is required
// before the Chief Executive or delegate can authorise, which is the list
// save rule in mapping.yaml.
//
// "Information requested" is this gate's ball-in-our-court member, and the
// "Response required" view tests it beside the ethics one.
Enum authorisation_status {
  "Not yet submitted"
  "Submitted"
  "Under governance review"
  "Information requested"
  "Authorised"
  "Not authorised"
  "Suspended"
}

// THE ACCUMULATION ANSWER. What the project itself is doing, independent of
// either gate. The default view filters the two terminal members away, so a
// register ten years deep still opens on this year's work — which is the
// whole reason a single list is workable here where a lookup picker is not.
Enum project_stage {
  "Planning"
  "Underway"
  "Closed to recruitment"
  "Completed"
  "Discontinued"
}

// The status of the LATEST amendment only. There are no amendment rows: this
// column, its reference and its date are the collapsed trace of the most
// recent change to the approved protocol. Earlier amendments survive in
// Governance Notes and in the list's version history, and nowhere else.
Enum amendment_status {
  "Drafting"
  "Submitted"
  "Under review"
  "Information requested"
  "Approved"
  "Not approved"
  "Withdrawn"
}

Table Project {
  Id                      int                     [pk, increment]
  Title                   nvarchar                [not null, note: 'The project title, worded as the ethics application words it']
  ProjectType             project_type            [not null, default: 'Research', note: 'What kind of activity this is. Quality improvement and audit belong here too - the register records the decision about whether they needed review']
  Department              nvarchar                [not null, note: 'The department or service this project runs in']
  // Text, not a person column: on an externally reviewed project the
  // principal investigator is frequently at the partner site or a university
  // and has no account in this tenant.
  PrincipalInvestigator   nvarchar                [not null, note: 'The principal investigator, named as the ethics application names them. They may be at another organisation']
  SiteInvestigator        person                  [note: 'The person HERE who answers for this project at this site. Leave blank only while it is still being decided']
  ProtocolReference       nvarchar                [note: 'Your protocol number and version, e.g. "PROT-14 v2.1"']
  Summary                 richtext                [note: 'What the project does, in plain language. No participant information of any kind']

  // --- Review pathway -----------------------------------------------------
  EthicsPathway           ethics_pathway          [not null, default: 'Not yet determined', note: 'Which review pathway this project takes. Low or negligible risk projects may be reviewed outside a full committee meeting; a quality activity may need no ethics review at all, and recording that decision here is the point']
  ParticipantInvolvement  participant_involvement [note: 'What the project does with people, as a property of the design. This is one of the things that decides the pathway above']
  ReviewingHREC           nvarchar                [note: 'The partner Human Research Ethics Committee reviewing this project. Write the name the same way every time']

  // --- Gate 1: the partner committee --------------------------------------
  EthicsStatus            ethics_status           [not null, default: 'Not yet submitted', note: 'Where the ETHICS application sits. This is not the same thing as site authorisation below']
  EthicsReference         nvarchar                [note: 'The reference the committee itself uses for this project, e.g. "HREC/2026/PHS/123456"']
  SubmittedDate           date                    [note: 'The date the ethics application was submitted']
  EthicsDecisionDate      date                    [note: 'The date the committee decided. For a quality activity that needed no review, the date that determination was made']
  // The expiry sweep works this column.
  EthicsApprovalExpiry    date                    [note: 'When this ethics approval ends. Nothing computes it - a report or an extension moves it, and the sweep in 50-govern finds the ones that have not moved']
  ConditionsStatus        conditions_status       [not null, default: 'None', note: 'Whether the approval carried conditions and whether they have been met. Outstanding conditions are not the same as an approval that has not arrived']
  ApprovalConditions      richtext                [note: 'The conditions as the committee worded them, verbatim. Paraphrase is how a condition quietly stops being met']

  // --- Gate 2: this site --------------------------------------------------
  SiteAuthorisationStatus authorisation_status    [not null, default: 'Not yet submitted', note: 'Where the SITE-SPECIFIC ASSESSMENT sits. Ethics approval alone does not let a project start here']
  SSASubmittedDate        date                    [note: 'The date the site-specific assessment was lodged with research governance']
  AuthorisationDate       date                    [note: 'The date the Chief Executive or delegate authorised this project for this site']
  AuthorisationReference  nvarchar                [note: 'The site authorisation reference, if your governance office issues one']
  // A role, not a name. The individual changes; the delegation does not.
  AuthorisedBy            nvarchar                [note: 'The role that authorised it, e.g. "Chief Executive" or "Director of Medical Services". Name the role rather than the person']

  // --- Oversight, and the recurring facts collapsed onto the row ----------
  ProjectStage            project_stage           [not null, default: 'Planning', note: 'What the project itself is doing. Underway means it has started HERE - do not set it until both gates above are cleared. The default view hides the two terminal values, which is how this register stays usable as it accumulates']
  // The reporting sweep works this pair. Progress reports go to the
  // reviewing committee at least annually and continued approval depends on
  // them, but the DUE DATE is set by your committee rather than nationally -
  // some use the approval anniversary, some a fixed institutional date - so
  // the register stores a date you supply rather than deriving one from a
  // cadence it would have to guess. Sources in 50-govern/governance.md.
  NextReportDue           date                    [note: 'When the next report is due to the committee. Your committee sets this date']
  // Earns its column by making a stale Next Report Due diagnosable: a due
  // date in the past with a LATER submission behind it means somebody filed
  // the report and did not roll the date, which is a different problem from
  // a report nobody has written.
  LastReportSubmitted     date                    [note: 'The date the most recent report actually went to the committee. Blank until the first one does']

  // The latest amendment, as three columns rather than as a list. They
  // travel together on purpose: a reference with no status is a filing note,
  // and a status with no date cannot age.
  LatestAmendmentReference nvarchar               [note: 'The reference of the most recent amendment, as the committee numbers it, e.g. "Amendment 3"']
  LatestAmendmentStatus   amendment_status        [note: 'Where the most recent amendment sits with the committee. Blank means this project has never been amended']
  LatestAmendmentDate     date                    [note: 'The date the most recent amendment was submitted or decided, whichever is later']
  // The only honest replacement for the amendment rows this design gives up.
  // Without it a reader who sees one amendment reference cannot tell whether
  // that is the only amendment or the fifth - which is exactly the fact the
  // collapse destroys.
  AmendmentCount          int                     [not null, default: 0, note: 'How many amendments this project has had in total, including the one named above. Bump it each time you record a new amendment']

  // The append-style history note. SharePoint has a true append-only Note
  // field and this is NOT it - the deployer sets AppendOnly false on every
  // multi-line column - so the append discipline is a governance convention
  // over an ordinary rich-text column, backed by list versioning. Said
  // plainly here because a column that merely looks append-only is worse
  // than one that does not pretend.
  GovernanceNotes         richtext                [note: 'The history of this project, newest entry first, each one dated and initialled: amendments, minute references, who chased what, why a date moved. This is where the amendments and reports that have no rows of their own survive']
  CompletedDate           date                    [note: 'The date the project finished or was discontinued at this site']

  // --- Calculated ---------------------------------------------------------
  // THE ANSWER, and no lookup pays for it: both gates are Choice columns on
  // this table, which a SharePoint calculated column can read. The formula
  // lives in mapping.yaml.
  //
  // It cannot know that an approval expired yesterday - a calculated column
  // may not reference [Today], which the build refuses. The overdue-date
  // colouring on the two deadline columns is the live half; the expiry sweep
  // in 50-govern is what moves Ethics Status to Expired and makes this
  // column tell the truth again.
  SiteReadiness           calculated_text         [note: 'Calculated automatically from the two gates above: whether this project can actually start here']

  // Exactly the columns a declared view filters on, and nothing else. Four
  // of twenty slots, and every declared filter is served past the list view
  // threshold - including the OR in "Response required", because an OR is
  // only narrowable when EVERY branch is indexed and both of its branches
  // are. Ethics Approval Expiry is deliberately NOT indexed: no view filters
  // it, the expiry sweep works the default view, and an index that serves
  // nothing is a slot spent. The calculated column cannot be indexed by
  // SharePoint at all and is rejected by the validator.
  indexes {
    EthicsStatus
    SiteAuthorisationStatus
    ProjectStage
    NextReportDue
  }
}
